#!/bin/sh
# rec-led - light the red LED while prudynt records video to the SD card
# (docs/recording.md). Install to /usr/sbin/rec-led (chmod +x); started by
# /etc/init.d/S96rec-led.
#
# prudynt's recorder keeps /run/prudynt/mp4ctl-ch<N>.active for as long as it
# writes a file, whichever way it was started (web UI Rec button, the `record`
# command, autostart at boot). This polls that once a second and drives one
# kernel LED channel: on while any channel records, off otherwise, off on exit.
# Only the red channel is touched, so Thingino's own `led` command and other
# users of the blue LED are left alone. No prudynt change, nothing when the
# recorder is idle.

FLAGS="/run/prudynt/mp4ctl-ch*.active"
LED="${REC_LED:-/sys/class/leds/led_r}"
INTERVAL="${REC_LED_INTERVAL:-1}"

if [ ! -d "$LED" ]; then
	logger -p daemon.err -t rec-led "no LED channel at $LED, exiting"
	exit 1
fi
MAX=$(cat "$LED/max_brightness" 2>/dev/null)
case "$MAX" in
	'' | *[!0-9]*) MAX=1 ;;
esac

set_led() {
	# A kernel trigger (timer, mmc0, ...) would override brightness: take it off
	[ -w "$LED/trigger" ] && printf 'none\n' >"$LED/trigger"
	printf '%s\n' "$1" >"$LED/brightness"
}

state=""
cleanup() {
	[ "$state" = "on" ] && set_led 0
	exit 0
}
trap cleanup TERM INT

logger -p daemon.info -t rec-led "watching $FLAGS -> $LED"
while :; do
	want=off
	# shellcheck disable=SC2086 # the glob must expand
	for f in $FLAGS; do
		[ -f "$f" ] && want=on && break
	done
	if [ "$want" != "$state" ]; then
		if [ "$want" = "on" ]; then set_led "$MAX"; else set_led 0; fi
		# the first pass only makes sure the LED is off; no "recording off" for that
		if [ -n "$state" ] || [ "$want" = "on" ]; then
			logger -p daemon.info -t rec-led "recording $want"
		fi
		state=$want
	fi
	sleep "$INTERVAL"
done
